/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · The Human Body (batch z) */
WB.L("blood-types-explained", "body", "beginner", "Blood types: why your blood has a label",
  "For centuries, giving someone blood was a gamble that often killed them. Then a Viennese doctor mixed a few test tubes and discovered why.",
  [
    ["A deadly gamble", "Early transfusions often killed because nobody knew that human blood comes in incompatible types.",
      "In 1667 the French physician Jean-Baptiste Denys transfused lamb's blood into human patients. After one man died, the practice was banned in France and fell out of favour for well over a century. From 1818 the English obstetrician James Blundell tried again with human blood, notably for women bleeding heavily after childbirth.\n\nSome patients recovered remarkably. Others suffered fever, back pain, dark urine and death, and no one could predict which. The same procedure that rescued one dying patient killed the next, and the reason lay hidden in the blood itself, invisible to the microscopes of the day."],
    ["Landsteiner's test tubes", "In 1901 Karl Landsteiner showed that some people's blood clumps when mixed, and so identified the blood groups.",
      "In 1901 Karl Landsteiner, working in Vienna, separated blood from himself and his colleagues into red cells and serum, the liquid part. He then mixed each person's serum with every other person's red cells. Sometimes nothing happened. Sometimes the cells clumped into sticky clusters, a reaction called agglutination.\n\nFrom the pattern he sorted people into groups, which became known as A, B and O. A year later two colleagues found the rarer group AB. The clumping explained the old disasters: incompatible donor cells were attacked and destroyed inside the patient. Landsteiner's work made safe transfusion possible and earned him the 1930 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],
    ["Labels on your red cells", "Your ABO type depends on which sugar markers coat your red cells, and your plasma carries antibodies against the ones you lack.",
      "Every red blood cell is studded with surface molecules. In the ABO system the key ones are chains of sugars called antigens. Type A cells carry the A antigen, type B cells carry B, type AB cells carry both, and type O cells carry neither.\n\nThe twist is that your plasma usually contains antibodies against whichever antigens you lack. Type A blood has anti-B antibodies, and type O has both anti-A and anti-B. They appear in early childhood, probably because gut bacteria carry similar sugars that train the immune system. If incompatible blood is transfused, these antibodies latch onto the foreign cells and can trigger a severe, sometimes fatal, reaction."],
    ["Positive and negative", "The Rh factor is a second blood group system, and a mismatch between mother and baby can cause serious disease.",
      "The plus or minus after your blood type refers to the Rh system, discovered in 1940 by Landsteiner and Alexander Wiener. They named it after the rhesus monkeys used in their experiments, though the human antigen later proved to be different. People whose cells carry the main Rh antigen, called D, are Rh positive. Those without it are Rh negative.\n\nThis matters in pregnancy. If an Rh-negative mother carries an Rh-positive baby, she can make antibodies that attack the red cells of that baby or a later one, a condition called haemolytic disease of the newborn. Since the late 1960s, injections of anti-D antibodies have prevented most cases."],
    ["Universal donors and more", "O negative red cells suit almost anyone, but there are over 40 blood group systems, and blood type does not shape personality.",
      "Type O negative red cells carry no A, B or D antigens, so a recipient's antibodies have nothing to attack. That is why emergency teams rely on O negative when there is no time to test. AB positive people can receive red cells of any type, and for plasma the rules flip: AB plasma has no ABO antibodies and suits anyone. Hospitals still crossmatch whenever they can.\n\nResearchers have identified more than 40 blood group systems, so rare combinations sometimes need specially matched donors. In Australia roughly four in ten people are O positive, fewer than one in ten O negative, and about one per cent AB negative. Careful large studies find no meaningful link between blood type and personality, despite the popular idea in Japan."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why were early transfusions so unpredictable?", ["Doctors used needles that were too thick","Nobody knew blood groups could clash","Patients always rejected animal blood","Blood spoiled before it could be given"], 1, "The hidden incompatibility between blood types explained why one transfusion saved a life and the next killed."],
    [1, "What did Landsteiner's mixing experiments reveal?", ["All human blood is chemically identical","Blood always clots when left in a tube","Clumping revealed distinct blood groups","Serum is toxic to all red blood cells"], 2, "The clumping pattern let him sort people into groups, which explained the old transfusion disasters."],
    [2, "Why does a person with type A blood react badly to type B red cells?", ["Their plasma holds anti-B antibodies","Type B cells are too large to circulate","Type A blood lacks the platelets to cope","Type B cells carry no antigens at all"], 0, "Plasma carries antibodies against the antigens you lack, and they attack incompatible cells."],
    [3, "What problem did anti-D injections largely prevent?", ["Blood clots after surgery","Iron deficiency in pregnancy","Allergic reactions to plasma","Haemolytic disease of the newborn"], 3, "Anti-D injections stop an Rh-negative mother from making antibodies against an Rh-positive baby's red cells."],
    [4, "Why is O negative blood used when there is no time to test?", ["It clots faster than other types","Its red cells lack A, B and D antigens","It carries more oxygen per cell","It is the most common type in Australia"], 1, "With no A, B or D antigens, O negative red cells rarely trigger a reaction in the recipient."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A few test tubes in Vienna explained why giving blood once killed patients."});

WB.L("how-wounds-heal", "body", "beginner", "How a cut heals",
  "Slice your finger and your body launches a carefully timed rescue operation, from emergency plugging to months of quiet rebuilding.",
  [
    ["Stopping the leak", "Within seconds of a cut, blood vessels tighten and platelets and fibrin plug the hole, forming a clot that dries into a scab.",
      "The moment skin is cut, damaged blood vessels tighten to reduce blood flow. Tiny cell fragments called platelets touch the exposed tissue and become sticky. They clump into a soft plug and release chemicals that call in more platelets.\n\nAt the same time a chain of clotting proteins switches on, step by step, until fibrinogen is converted into long strands of fibrin. These strands weave through the plug like a net, trapping red cells and turning liquid blood into a solid clot. As the surface dries out, it becomes the familiar crusty scab."],
    ["The clean-up crew", "Redness, heat, swelling and pain are signs that your immune cells have arrived to clean the wound.",
      "Around two thousand years ago the Roman writer Celsus described the four classic signs of inflammation: redness, heat, swelling and pain. They do not mean something is going wrong. Damaged cells release chemical alarms that widen nearby blood vessels, bringing extra blood that makes the area red and warm. Leakier vessel walls let fluid and immune cells seep into the tissue, causing swelling.\n\nWithin hours, white blood cells called neutrophils arrive to kill bacteria. Macrophages follow, large cells that swallow debris, dead cells and germs. They also signal the next wave of repair cells to start work, which makes them the managers of healing."],
    ["Rebuilding the tissue", "Over days, fibroblasts lay down collagen, new capillaries sprout and skin cells crawl across to close the wound.",
      "After a few days the wound enters its building phase. Cells called fibroblasts move in and make collagen, the tough protein that gives skin its strength. New capillaries sprout to supply oxygen and nutrients. Together they form granulation tissue, the pink, slightly bumpy surface you may see in a healing graze.\n\nMeanwhile, skin cells at the edges multiply and crawl across the gap, sliding under the scab until they meet in the middle. Some fibroblasts turn into cells that contract, gently pulling the wound edges closer. Once the new skin is complete, the scab is no longer needed and falls away."],
    ["The long remodel", "Scars keep remodelling for a year or more, but never fully match the original skin.",
      "The final phase is the slowest. The first collagen is laid down hastily. Over following months the body breaks it down and replaces it with stronger, better organised fibres. A scar that looked red and raised often fades and flattens over a year or more.\n\nEven so, scar tissue never becomes perfect skin. It typically regains at most around 80 per cent of the original strength, and it usually lacks hair follicles and sweat glands. Curiously, researchers have found that wounds in early foetuses can heal without any scar at all. Understanding how is a long-standing goal of wound research."],
    ["Care and what slows healing", "Keep small cuts clean and covered, and know that smoking, diabetes, poor nutrition and infection slow repair.",
      "Many of us were told to let cuts breathe. In 1962 British researcher George Winter tested this on pigs, whose skin is similar to ours. Shallow wounds covered with plastic film healed considerably faster than wounds left in the air. A dry scab is a barrier that new skin cells must tunnel under, while a moist surface lets them glide across. So clean small cuts gently and keep them covered.\n\nHealing needs blood supply, oxygen and raw materials. Smoking narrows blood vessels, diabetes can damage circulation, and collagen needs protein and vitamin C. Healing also slows with age. Seek medical help for wounds that are deep, gaping, very dirty, caused by animal bites, or showing spreading infection. For severe bleeding, apply firm pressure and call 000."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What forms the net of strands that turns liquid blood into a clot?", ["Collagen","Keratin","Haemoglobin","Fibrin"], 3, "Fibrinogen is converted into fibrin strands, which weave through the platelet plug and trap red cells."],
    [1, "Why does an injured area become red and warm?", ["Bacteria produce heat as they grow","Nearby vessels widen and carry extra blood","Nerves overheat from the pain signals","The scab traps heat inside the wound"], 1, "Inflammation is the immune response at work, not a sign that healing has failed."],
    [2, "Which cells make the collagen that rebuilds the wound?", ["Neutrophils","Platelets","Fibroblasts","Macrophages"], 2, "Fibroblasts produce collagen, which forms granulation tissue and gives the new skin its strength."],
    [3, "How does a mature scar compare with the original skin?", ["It is stronger than the original skin","It matches the original once healed","It grows extra hair and sweat glands","It is weaker and lacks hair follicles"], 3, "Scar tissue regains at most about 80 per cent of the original strength and lacks follicles and sweat glands."],
    [4, "What did George Winter's 1962 pig experiment suggest for small cuts?", ["Wounds heal fastest in direct sunlight","Letting a scab form speeds healing","Covered wounds heal faster than exposed ones","Covering wounds usually causes infection"], 2, "A moist, covered wound lets new skin cells glide across, while a dry scab forces them to tunnel."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Leaving a cut open to the air feels sensible. A 1962 experiment on pigs says otherwise."});

WB.L("teeth-enamel-decay", "body", "beginner", "Your teeth: the hardest part of you",
  "Your teeth are coated in the toughest material your body makes, yet a bag of lollies can slowly dissolve it. Here is how they work and how to keep them.",
  [
    ["Built in layers", "Enamel is the hardest substance in the body, but it has no living cells, so lost enamel cannot regrow.",
      "A tooth is built like a fortress. The outer layer of the crown is enamel, the hardest substance in the human body. It is about 96 per cent mineral, mostly crystals of a calcium phosphate called hydroxyapatite. Beneath it lies dentine, softer and full of microscopic tubes, which is why a worn or cracked tooth can feel sensitive to cold.\n\nAt the centre is the pulp, a soft core of nerves and blood vessels that keeps the tooth alive. Tiny fibres called the periodontal ligament anchor the root into the jawbone. Enamel has one great weakness: it contains no living cells, so once it is lost the body cannot grow it back."],
    ["Two sets for life", "Humans get only two sets of teeth, 20 baby teeth then 32 adult teeth, which must last a lifetime.",
      "Like most mammals, we get two sets of teeth. Children have 20 baby teeth, which usually start appearing at around six months, while the adult teeth are already forming deep in the jaw. From about age six the baby teeth loosen and fall out as permanent teeth push up from below.\n\nA full adult set has 32 teeth: incisors for cutting, canines for tearing, premolars and molars for grinding. The last four molars, the wisdom teeth, usually arrive between the late teens and mid twenties, if they arrive at all. Unlike sharks, which replace teeth throughout their lives, we get no third set."],
    ["How decay happens", "Mouth bacteria turn sugar into acid that dissolves enamel, so how often you eat sugar matters more than how much.",
      "Your mouth is home to hundreds of species of bacteria, living in a sticky film called plaque. Some, including Streptococcus mutans, feed on sugars and release acid as waste. When a tooth surface becomes acidic enough, roughly below a pH of 5.5, minerals begin to dissolve out of the enamel.\n\nSaliva fights back. It neutralises acid and supplies calcium and phosphate that can rebuild early damage. Trouble starts when attacks come too often for saliva to keep up. That is why how often you eat sugar matters more than the total amount. Sipping soft drink all afternoon is harder on teeth than having one with lunch."],
    ["The brown stain mystery", "Fluoride's protective power was discovered through brown-stained teeth that rarely decayed, and it helps enamel resist acid.",
      "In the early 1900s a young dentist named Frederick McKay moved to Colorado Springs and noticed many locals had brown-stained teeth. Over years he found those teeth were strangely resistant to decay, and the cause was eventually traced to naturally high fluoride in the water. Researchers then worked out that a much lower level could reduce decay without staining.\n\nIn 1945 Grand Rapids, Michigan, became the first city to add fluoride to its water deliberately. In Australia, the Tasmanian town of Beaconsfield led the way in 1953. Fluoride helps enamel rebuild into a form called fluorapatite, which resists acid better than the original."],
    ["Gums and daily care", "Plaque left along the gumline can lead to gum disease and tooth loss, so brush twice daily with fluoride toothpaste and clean between teeth.",
      "Healthy teeth can still be lost if the gums and bone fail. Plaque left along the gumline causes gingivitis: redness, swelling and bleeding when you brush. At this stage good cleaning reverses it. Left in place, plaque hardens into tartar, which a toothbrush cannot remove. Ongoing inflammation can progress to periodontitis, where pockets form and the supporting bone is gradually destroyed, so teeth loosen and may fall out. Gum disease has been linked with diabetes and heart disease, though how much one causes the other is still studied.\n\nPeople have cleaned teeth for thousands of years, from chew sticks to hog-hair brushes, and nylon bristles arrived in 1938. Australian dental authorities recommend brushing twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, cleaning between teeth, and spitting rather than rinsing so some fluoride stays on the enamel."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can't the body repair enamel that has been lost?", ["Enamel is made of protein","Enamel is replaced every year","Enamel contains no living cells","Enamel has no minerals"], 2, "Enamel is non-living mineral, so once it is lost the body cannot regrow it."],
    [1, "How many teeth are in a full adult set?", ["32","20","28","36"], 0, "Humans get 20 baby teeth and then 32 adult teeth, with no third set."],
    [2, "Why is sipping a sugary drink all afternoon worse than having it with a meal?", ["Sugar becomes more acidic the longer it sits","Saliva gets no break to repair the enamel","Saliva stops flowing once a meal has finished","The sugar stains the teeth permanently brown"], 1, "The frequency of sugar exposure matters because saliva needs gaps between acid attacks to rebuild enamel."],
    [3, "What clue led to the discovery of fluoride's benefits?", ["Teeth that glowed under ultraviolet light","Children who never lost baby teeth","Sailors whose teeth fell out","Brown-stained teeth that rarely decayed"], 3, "Naturally fluoridated water produced stained but decay-resistant teeth, which showed fluoride protects enamel."],
    [4, "What is true of gingivitis?", ["It always leads to tooth loss","Good cleaning can reverse it","It is caused by too much fluoride","It only affects people over sixty"], 1, "Early gum disease reverses with good cleaning, but untreated plaque can progress to periodontitis."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your teeth are coated in the hardest thing you make, and it can never regrow."});

WB.L("how-your-voice-works", "body", "beginner", "How your voice works",
  "Every word you speak begins as a buzz from two tiny folds of tissue in your throat. The rest is shaped by your body like a musical instrument.",
  [
    ["The voice box", "Your voice is made in the larynx, where two vocal folds sit across the airway and vibrate when you speak.",
      "Sitting at the top of your windpipe is the larynx, or voice box, a structure of cartilage, muscle and ligament. The bump at the front of the neck, the Adam's apple, is part of its largest cartilage and tends to stick out more in men.\n\nStretched across the airway are two vocal folds. They are often called vocal cords, but they are not strings. Each is a layered band of muscle and ligament covered in soft, moist membrane. When you breathe, the folds swing apart to let air through. When you swallow, they snap shut, helping stop food and drink going down the wrong way. When you speak, they come together and vibrate."],
    ["Making the buzz", "Air from the lungs makes the vocal folds open and shut hundreds of times a second, and tighter folds give higher pitch.",
      "To make sound, muscles bring the vocal folds together. Air pushed up from the lungs builds pressure beneath them until it forces them apart and a puff escapes. The folds spring back together, helped by their own elasticity and the pressure drop as air rushes between them, and the cycle repeats.\n\nIt happens remarkably fast. A typical adult man's speaking voice vibrates at around 100 to 120 cycles per second, and a typical woman's at around 200. Tighter, thinner folds vibrate faster and make higher notes, which is how you change pitch. Push more air through and the sound gets louder. On its own, though, this buzz sounds nothing like speech."],
    ["Shaping the sound", "Your throat, mouth and nose shape the raw buzz into speech, which is why you can whisper without vibrating your vocal folds.",
      "The raw buzz travels up through the throat, mouth and nose, which together form the vocal tract. Like a guitar's body, this space strengthens some frequencies and weakens others. By moving your tongue, lips, jaw and soft palate you change its shape, and with it the sound. Vowels depend mainly on tongue and lip position: say \"ee\" then \"oo\" and feel the difference. Consonants come from briefly blocking or narrowing airflow with the lips, teeth or tongue.\n\nWhispering shows the two systems working separately. Your vocal folds do not vibrate at all, yet you still form clear words using the air rushing through your mouth."],
    ["Why voices change", "Puberty, hormones and ageing reshape the larynx, which is why voices deepen, crack and change across life.",
      "Children of both sexes have similar voices because their larynxes are similar in size. At puberty, testosterone makes the larynx grow and the vocal folds lengthen and thicken, especially in boys. Longer, heavier folds vibrate more slowly, and many male voices drop by around an octave. During the change a voice may crack unpredictably as the muscles adjust to a rapidly changing instrument.\n\nGirls' voices deepen too, but only slightly. Voices keep changing throughout life. In older age, vocal folds can thin and lose some muscle, making a voice weaker or breathier. Hormonal changes around menopause can also lower a woman's voice a little."],
    ["Hearing and caring for it", "Your recorded voice sounds odd because you normally hear yourself through bone, and lasting hoarseness needs a doctor.",
      "Most people wince at a recording of their own voice. Others hear you only through the air, but you also hear yourself through the bones of your skull. Bone conduction carries low frequencies well, so the voice in your head sounds deeper and fuller. A recording captures only the airborne version, which is much closer to what others hear.\n\nBecause the folds collide many times a second, heavy use takes a toll. Teachers, singers, coaches and call centre workers are most at risk of voice problems, including swelling or small callus-like growths called vocal nodules. Rest a sore voice, avoid habitual shouting, stay hydrated and do not smoke. Whispering is not necessarily restful. Hoarseness from a cold usually settles within a week or two, but if it lasts more than about three weeks, see a doctor."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do the vocal folds do when you swallow?", ["Vibrate rapidly to make sound","Stretch to their longest length","Close to help protect the airway","Swing wide open to let air in"], 2, "Closing the folds helps stop food and drink going down the wrong way."],
    [1, "How do you raise the pitch of your voice?", ["Push much more air through","Tighten and thin the vocal folds","Open your mouth wider","Breathe through your nose"], 1, "Tighter, thinner folds vibrate faster, producing higher notes, while more air mainly adds volume."],
    [2, "What does whispering show about how speech is made?", ["Words can form without folds vibrating","It is made using only the nasal passages","It needs no airflow from the lungs","The folds vibrate fastest when whispering"], 0, "Whispering separates the buzz from the shaping: the vocal tract alone forms clear words."],
    [3, "Why do many male voices drop at puberty?", ["The lungs grow larger and push more air","The tongue grows heavier and slows speech","The nose lengthens and adds resonance","Testosterone thickens and lengthens the folds"], 3, "Longer, heavier folds vibrate more slowly, producing a lower voice."],
    [4, "Why does your voice sound deeper to you than on a recording?", ["Microphones cut out the low notes of speech","You also hear yourself through skull bones","Recordings play back faster than real speech","Your ears are tuned to higher sounds only"], 1, "Bone conduction adds low frequencies that a recording, and everyone else, does not hear."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your own recorded voice sounds wrong to you. It is actually the accurate one."});

WB.L("water-and-thirst", "body", "beginner", "Water and thirst: how much do you really need?",
  "Eight glasses a day sounds like ancient wisdom, but its origins are murkier than you might think. Your body has a far better guide built in.",
  [
    ["A body made of water", "Water makes up roughly half to 60 per cent of an adult's body weight and underlies almost every process in the body.",
      "Water is the most abundant substance in your body. In a typical adult it makes up roughly 50 to 60 per cent of body weight, and in newborn babies around 75 per cent. People with more muscle tend to carry more water, because muscle holds more than fat does.\n\nAbout two thirds sits inside your cells, and the rest bathes the spaces between cells or flows as blood plasma. Water dissolves nutrients, carries waste to the kidneys, cushions joints and the brain, and spreads heat around the body. Almost every chemical reaction that keeps you alive happens in it, which is why your body guards its water levels so carefully."],
    ["Coming and going", "You lose water constantly through urine, sweat and breath, and food supplies roughly a fifth of what you take in.",
      "Even on a quiet day you are always losing water. Most leaves as urine, but a surprising amount escapes unnoticed as sweat evaporating from your skin and as vapour in every breath. More is lost in faeces. A sedentary adult in a mild climate typically loses around two and a half litres a day, and far more during hard exercise or hot weather.\n\nWater comes in from drinks, from food, and in a small amount from the chemical reactions that burn fuel in your cells. Food matters more than many people realise. Fruit, vegetables, soups, rice and pasta are full of water, and food can supply roughly a fifth of a typical intake."],
    ["The eight glasses myth", "There is no good evidence that everyone needs eight glasses of plain water a day, and all drinks count.",
      "The rule of eight glasses a day is repeated everywhere, yet nobody has found solid scientific evidence behind it. One often-cited origin is a 1945 recommendation from a United States nutrition board of about two and a half litres of water a day for adults. The same advice noted that most of this is already in prepared foods, a detail that was quietly forgotten.\n\nAustralian guidelines suggest typical fluid intakes from drinks of around 2.6 litres for men and 2.1 litres for women, but these are averages for healthy people, not targets everyone must hit. Tea, coffee, milk and other drinks all count. The mild diuretic effect of caffeine is small in regular drinkers."],
    ["Your built-in sensor", "Your brain tracks how concentrated your blood is and adjusts thirst and urine to match, so thirst and pale urine are a reliable guide.",
      "Deep in the brain, the hypothalamus holds sensors that track how concentrated your blood is. When you lose water, the blood becomes slightly saltier and these sensors respond in two ways. They make you feel thirsty, and they prompt the pituitary gland to release antidiuretic hormone, also called vasopressin.\n\nThis hormone tells the kidneys to reabsorb more water, so urine becomes smaller in volume and darker. When you drink plenty, the hormone falls and the kidneys release more dilute urine. The system responds to changes of only a few per cent. For most healthy people, drinking when thirsty and noticing pale straw-coloured urine is a reliable guide."],
    ["Too little, too much", "Older adults, babies and people working in heat are prone to dehydration, but drinking far more than you need can dangerously dilute blood sodium.",
      "Thirst works well for most healthy adults, but it weakens with age, so older people can become dehydrated without feeling thirsty. Babies and young children lose fluid quickly through fever, vomiting or diarrhoea and cannot fetch a drink. Hard work in Australian summer heat can sweat out more than a litre an hour. Early signs include thirst, dark urine, headache, tiredness and dizziness. Heat stroke is an emergency: if someone in the heat is confused or collapses, call 000 and start cooling them.\n\nDrinking too much is also risky. If fluid arrives faster than your kidneys can remove it, blood sodium becomes diluted (hyponatraemia) and brain cells can swell. A 2005 study found around one in eight Boston Marathon runners had low blood sodium at the finish. Many sports doctors now advise drinking to thirst."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly what share of an adult's body weight is water?", ["10 to 20 per cent","About 30 per cent","50 to 60 per cent","80 to 90 per cent"], 2, "Water makes up roughly half to 60 per cent of an adult's weight, and more of a newborn's."],
    [1, "Which source of water do people often overlook?", ["Air absorbed through the skin","Fruit, vegetables and soups","Water stored in the bones","Breathing in through the nose"], 1, "Food can supply roughly a fifth of a typical water intake."],
    [2, "What detail of the 1945 recommendation was later forgotten?", ["That it applied only to hot climates","That it was meant for children only","That most of it comes from food","That it counted only plain tap water"], 2, "The famous advice never meant eight glasses of plain water, since much of our water comes from food."],
    [3, "What does antidiuretic hormone do when you are low on water?", ["It tells the kidneys to hold on to water","It signals the skin to release more sweat","It tells the liver to store extra sugar","It makes the heart beat much faster"], 0, "The kidneys reabsorb more water, so urine becomes smaller in volume and darker."],
    [4, "What is the danger of drinking far more water than you need?", ["Blood sugar rises sharply","Blood sodium becomes dangerously diluted","The kidneys stop making urine","The blood becomes too concentrated"], 1, "Excess water dilutes blood sodium, which can swell brain cells, so drinking to thirst is usually safer."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Eight glasses a day is everywhere, yet nobody can show where it came from."});

WB.L("fever-explained", "body", "intermediate", "Fever: why your body turns up the heat",
  "A fever is not your body losing control of its temperature. It is a deliberate decision, and one that animals have been making for hundreds of millions of years.",
  [
    ["Resetting the thermostat", "In a fever the brain deliberately raises its target temperature, which is why you shiver and feel cold as it rises.",
      "Your body temperature is controlled by the hypothalamus, a small region at the base of the brain that works like a thermostat. During an infection, immune cells release signalling molecules, including cytokines such as interleukin 1 and interleukin 6. These trigger a substance called prostaglandin E2, which raises the hypothalamus's set point.\n\nSuddenly your normal temperature feels too cold. You shiver, your skin turns pale as blood vessels narrow to save heat, and you reach for a blanket, all to warm up to the new target. When the fever breaks and the set point drops, the opposite happens: you feel hot, flush and sweat heavily."],
    ["The myth of 37 degrees", "Normal body temperature is a range that shifts through the day, and 37 degrees is only an approximate average.",
      "The figure of 37 degrees Celsius traces largely to German physician Carl Wunderlich, who in 1868 published an analysis of a huge number of armpit readings. His work helped make the thermometer a standard medical tool. Modern measurements suggest the average is often a little lower, commonly around 36.5 to 36.8 degrees, depending on the person and where it is measured.\n\nYour temperature also follows your body clock, usually lowest in the early morning and highest in the late afternoon or evening. Age, activity and, for women, the menstrual cycle shift it too. Some researchers have found evidence that average temperatures have drifted down since Wunderlich's day."],
    ["Lizards with a fever", "Cold-blooded animals give themselves a fever by seeking warmth, which suggests fever is ancient and useful.",
      "In the 1970s physiologist Matthew Kluger studied desert iguanas, which cannot make their own body heat and control temperature by moving between warm and cool spots. When infected with bacteria, the lizards chose warmer places, raising their temperature by around two degrees, a so-called behavioural fever. Lizards kept at cooler temperatures were much more likely to die.\n\nSimilar behaviour has since been seen in fish, insects and other animals. Because fever appears across so many branches of the animal kingdom, and costs a lot of energy, most scientists conclude it evolved because it helps fight infection. A trait this costly and this useless would probably have been lost long ago."],
    ["How heat helps", "Higher temperatures speed up parts of the immune system and make life harder for some germs.",
      "Fever seems to help in several ways. Many immune processes run faster when warm, including the movement of white blood cells and the production of some defensive molecules. Some bacteria and viruses multiply less efficiently in warmer surroundings, and the body also restricts the iron many bacteria need to grow.\n\nHistory offers a grim illustration. In 1917 Austrian psychiatrist Julius Wagner-Jauregg deliberately infected patients with neurosyphilis, then an incurable brain infection, with malaria to induce high fevers. Some improved, and he won the Nobel Prize in 1927. The treatment was dangerous and was abandoned once penicillin arrived, but it showed that fever could attack a disease."],
    ["Fever, heat stroke, and help", "A fever is regulated by the brain, whereas heat stroke is the body failing to shed heat, and some fevers need urgent care.",
      "In a fever the brain has raised its set point and the body still regulates around it, so infection fevers rarely climb much above about 41 degrees. In heat stroke the set point is unchanged: the body absorbs or makes heat faster than it can lose it, and temperature keeps rising. Fever medicines act on the set point, so they do not treat heat stroke. It is an emergency: call 000 and start cooling the person.\n\nMost fevers pass within a few days, and medicines mainly improve comfort, so follow the label or ask a pharmacist. Get urgent help for a baby under three months with fever, or for anyone with a stiff neck, a rash that does not fade when pressed, trouble breathing, severe drowsiness or confusion. Call 000 if a febrile seizure lasts more than five minutes."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does a person with a rising fever shiver and feel cold?", ["Their blood has lost heat to the infection","The brain has raised its target temperature","The infection has damaged their nerves","Their muscles are too weak to stay warm"], 1, "The raised set point makes normal body temperature feel too cold, so the body works to warm up."],
    [1, "What do modern measurements say about normal body temperature?", ["It is exactly 37 degrees for everyone","It never changes during the day","It is always highest in the morning","It is a range, often just under 37"], 3, "Normal temperature varies by person, time of day and measurement site, and 37 degrees is only an old average."],
    [2, "What did the desert iguana experiments suggest about fever?", ["Warmth-seeking lizards survived better","Lizards never become infected by bacteria","Fever is deadly for cold-blooded animals","Infected lizards moved to cooler spots"], 0, "Fever across the animal kingdom, even self-induced, suggests it is an ancient and useful defence."],
    [3, "How does a higher temperature help fight infection?", ["It destroys all bacteria within minutes","It switches off immune cells to save energy","It makes the skin thicker to block germs","It speeds immune cells and slows some germs"], 3, "Warmth boosts parts of the immune response and makes conditions harder for some microbes."],
    [4, "Why don't fever-reducing medicines treat heat stroke?", ["They only work on young children","The set point is not raised in heat stroke","They wear off too quickly in the heat","Heat stroke comes from a bacterial infection"], 1, "Heat stroke is the body failing to shed heat, so it needs urgent cooling and a call to 000."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A fever is not your body losing control. It is closer to the opposite."});

WB.L("reflexes-spinal-cord", "body", "intermediate", "Reflexes: when your spine decides first",
  "Touch a hot pan and your hand is gone before you feel the pain. That split-second move is made without your brain's permission.",
  [
    ["Descartes and the fire", "The idea that the body can react automatically goes back to Descartes, and we now know many reflexes are handled by the spinal cord.",
      "René Descartes' Treatise of Man, published in 1664 after his death, included a famous drawing of a boy with his foot near a fire. Descartes imagined that heat tugged on tiny threads in the nerves, opening valves in the brain that sent spirits to the muscles to pull the foot away. The details were wrong, but the core idea was striking: some actions might happen mechanically, like clockwork, without any conscious choice.\n\nOver the following centuries, scientists found that many automatic responses do not even need the brain. They are handled lower down, in the spinal cord, by circuits we now call reflex arcs."],
    ["The knee jerk", "The knee jerk is a simple reflex in which a stretch sensor connects directly to a motor neuron in the spinal cord.",
      "When a doctor taps the tendon below your kneecap, your lower leg kicks forward. In 1875 two German neurologists, Wilhelm Erb and Carl Westphal, independently described this reflex, and it has been tested ever since.\n\nThe tap briefly stretches the quadriceps muscle at the front of the thigh. Stretch sensors in the muscle, called muscle spindles, fire signals along a sensory nerve into the spinal cord. There, the sensory neuron connects directly to a motor neuron, which sends a command straight back, telling the muscle to contract. With only one connection in the chain, it is a monosynaptic reflex. In everyday life it helps keep you upright."],
    ["Anatomy of a reflex", "Every reflex arc has a receptor, a sensory neuron, a processing step, a motor neuron and a muscle or gland, and muscles work in coordinated pairs.",
      "A reflex arc has five basic parts. A receptor detects a change such as stretch, heat or pressure. A sensory neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord or brainstem. A processing step links it to an outgoing motor neuron, sometimes through extra cells called interneurons. Finally a muscle or gland responds.\n\nBritish physiologist Charles Sherrington spent decades mapping these circuits and coined the word synapse for the gap between nerve cells. He showed that reflexes coordinate muscles in pairs. When the quadriceps contracts in the knee jerk, signals simultaneously relax the hamstrings behind the thigh so they do not fight the movement. Sherrington shared the 1932 Nobel Prize."],
    ["Pulling back from danger", "The withdrawal reflex pulls you away from harm and stiffens your other leg, often before you feel any pain.",
      "Step on a sharp object and your foot lifts before you have time to think. This withdrawal reflex is more complex than the knee jerk. It uses several interneurons in the spinal cord to activate a group of muscles that pull the limb away.\n\nAt the same moment, a linked circuit called the crossed extensor reflex stiffens your other leg so it can take your weight and you do not topple. Signals also travel up to the brain, which is why you then feel the pain, look down and perhaps say something unprintable. The reflex circuit is short and conscious pain takes longer to form, so the movement often comes first."],
    ["Babies, speed and skill", "Reflexes take tens of milliseconds, far quicker than conscious reactions, and newborn reflexes help doctors check development.",
      "Newborn reflexes fade within months as the brain's higher regions take control. Stroke a cheek and the baby turns to feed; touch a palm and they grip; sense a fall and they fling out their arms. In 1896 Joseph Babinski described how stroking the sole makes the big toe curl down in healthy adults. If it points up and the toes fan out, it can signal damage to nerve pathways from the brain.\n\nA spinal reflex takes only a few tens of milliseconds. A conscious reaction to something you see takes around a fifth to a quarter of a second. In sprinting, a runner who reacts within a tenth of a second of the gun has false started, because a genuine response cannot reliably be that fast. Catching a ball feels like a reflex but is learned."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do scientists now know about many automatic responses?", ["They all need the brain to process them","Many are handled in the spinal cord","They are learned during early childhood","They are controlled by the cerebellum alone"], 1, "Reflex arcs in the spinal cord handle many automatic responses without involving the brain."],
    [1, "In the knee-jerk reflex, what does the sensory neuron connect to in the spinal cord?", ["A long chain of brain cells","The heart's pacemaker","A motor neuron directly","The bones of the knee"], 2, "Only one connection links the sensor to the motor neuron, which makes the knee jerk monosynaptic and very fast."],
    [2, "What happens to the hamstrings when the quadriceps contracts in the knee jerk?", ["They contract along with it","They are cut off from the brain","They shorten and stiffen up","They relax rather than resist"], 3, "Reflexes coordinate muscles in pairs so that opposing muscles do not fight each other."],
    [3, "What does the crossed extensor reflex do when you step on something sharp?", ["It braces the other leg to take your weight","It sends the pain signal to the brain faster","It lifts both of your feet off the ground","It slows your heart rate to calm you"], 0, "It keeps you balanced while the injured foot withdraws, often before you feel pain."],
    [4, "Why is a sprinter who reacts within a tenth of a second counted as false starting?", ["Reflexes are banned in athletics","Genuine reactions are not that fast","The gun's sound takes longer to arrive","It gives other runners a disadvantage"], 1, "Conscious reactions take around a fifth of a second or more, so anything much faster is treated as anticipation."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Touch something hot and your hand moves before you feel it. Who decided?"});

WB.L("lymphatic-system", "body", "intermediate", "The lymphatic system: your hidden drainage network",
  "Alongside your blood vessels runs a second network that most people barely know exists. Without it, you would swell up like a balloon.",
  [
    ["The leak problem", "Fluid constantly leaks out of your capillaries, and the lymphatic system collects it and returns it to the blood.",
      "Your smallest blood vessels, the capillaries, are deliberately leaky. As blood flows through, pressure pushes plasma into the tissues, carrying oxygen and nutrients to cells. Most of it seeps back, but not all. Each day several litres are left among the cells. If nothing collected it, your tissues would swell and your blood volume would steadily fall.\n\nThat is the job of the lymphatic system, a network of thin-walled vessels that begin as tiny dead-end tubes woven among the tissues. They soak up the excess fluid, now called lymph, along with proteins, debris and sometimes germs, and carry it back towards the bloodstream."],
    ["A system with no pump", "Lymph is moved by muscle movement, breathing and one-way valves rather than by a heart.",
      "Blood has the heart to push it around, but lymph has no central pump. Lymphatic vessels contain one-way valves, like those in veins, that stop fluid flowing backwards. The walls of larger vessels can contract rhythmically, and each time nearby muscles squeeze, or your chest rises and falls as you breathe, lymph is nudged further along.\n\nMost of it eventually drains through the thoracic duct into large veins near the left collarbone, rejoining the blood. This helps explain why ankles often swell after hours of sitting still on a long flight. With the leg muscles inactive, fluid moves along more slowly, and gravity lets it pool in the feet."],
    ["Checkpoints for germs", "Hundreds of lymph nodes filter lymph and act as meeting points where immune cells encounter signs of infection.",
      "Along the lymphatic vessels sit lymph nodes, small bean-shaped structures found in clusters in places such as the neck, armpits and groin. Estimates vary, but a typical adult has several hundred. As lymph passes through, the nodes filter out debris and microbes. They are also packed with lymphocytes, the immune cells that recognise specific invaders.\n\nBecause fluid from every tissue flows through them, nodes act as checkpoints where immune cells meet evidence of infection. When they detect a threat, lymphocytes multiply rapidly, which is why the glands in your neck can swell and become tender during a sore throat. The tonsils, spleen and thymus are also part of the wider lymphatic family."],
    ["Milky vessels and fat", "Dietary fat enters the body through lymphatic vessels called lacteals, which an Italian anatomist first spotted in a dog in 1622.",
      "In 1622 Italian anatomist Gaspare Aselli, dissecting a recently fed dog, noticed thin white threads across its intestines. Cutting one released a milky fluid. He called them lacteals, from the Latin for milk. Decades later Jean Pecquet traced the fluid to the thoracic duct, and in the 1650s Olaus Rudbeck and Thomas Bartholin described the wider lymphatic network.\n\nAselli's vessels had a real purpose. Most nutrients go straight into blood vessels leading to the liver, but fats are packaged into large particles called chylomicrons that enter the lacteals instead. After a fatty meal, gut lymph turns cloudy and white. This fat-rich fluid travels up the thoracic duct and enters the bloodstream near the collarbone, reaching the body before it passes through the liver."],
    ["When drainage fails", "Damage to lymph vessels causes long-lasting swelling called lymphoedema, and even the brain turned out to have lymphatic vessels.",
      "When lymphatic vessels are blocked or damaged, fluid builds up as lymphoedema, a persistent swelling often in an arm or leg. In Australia and similar countries a common cause is cancer treatment, because surgery or radiation may damage lymph nodes. Cancers can spread via lymph vessels, so surgeons often check the first node a tumour drains into, called the sentinel node. In parts of the tropics, mosquito-borne parasitic worms cause lymphatic filariasis, which in severe cases brings enormous swelling called elephantiasis.\n\nTextbooks long taught that the brain had no lymphatic vessels. In 2015 two teams, one in the United States and one in Finland, independently reported them in the meninges, the membranes wrapping the brain. They appear to drain fluid, waste and immune cells towards nodes in the neck. Their role in disease remains uncertain."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does the body need a lymphatic system?", ["To pump blood while the heart rests","To make most of the body's red blood cells","To return fluid that leaks from capillaries","To carry oxygen to the brain's tissues"], 2, "Without lymphatic drainage, leaked fluid would pool in tissues and blood volume would fall."],
    [1, "What moves lymph through the body?", ["A second small heart in the chest","Muscle squeezes, breathing and valves","Suction created by the kidneys","Gravity pulling it downhill alone"], 1, "With no central pump, squeezing muscles, breathing and valves keep lymph flowing one way."],
    [2, "Why can glands in your neck swell during a throat infection?", ["Fat collects around the neck during illness","The thyroid gland always becomes infected","Small blood vessels in the neck burst open","Lymph node cells multiply to fight the germs"], 3, "Lymph nodes are checkpoints where lymphocytes multiply when they detect a threat."],
    [3, "How does fat from a meal first enter circulation?", ["Directly into the liver's blood supply","Through lacteals in the intestine walls","Through the lining of the stomach","By being absorbed in the large bowel"], 1, "Fat particles are too large for blood capillaries, so they travel in the lymph before joining the blood."],
    [4, "What did researchers report in 2015?", ["The brain was proved to lack lymphatic vessels","Lymph nodes were found to make red blood cells","Lymphatic vessels were found around the brain","The thoracic duct was found to end in the brain"], 2, "The discovery overturned textbook teaching and may help explain how the brain clears waste."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"You have a second circulation with no pump, and the brain's was missed for centuries."});

WB.L("vestigial-body-parts", "body", "intermediate", "Evolution's leftovers: goosebumps, tailbones and the appendix",
  "Your body carries traces of ancestors who had fur, tails and much bigger jaws. Some leftovers have found new jobs; others are just along for the ride.",
  [
    ["What vestigial means", "A vestigial structure has lost its original function, or much of it, but it is not necessarily useless.",
      "In The Descent of Man, published in 1871, Charles Darwin pointed to what he called rudimentary organs as evidence that humans share ancestry with other animals. These were parts that seemed shrunken or purposeless in us but were fully functional in other species.\n\nThe modern term is vestigial. It is often misunderstood as meaning completely useless. Biologists use it more carefully: a vestigial structure has lost its original function, or much of it, though it may have gained a new one. In the late 1800s some anatomists drew up long lists of supposedly useless organs. Some items on those lists, such as the thymus, later proved essential."],
    ["Why we get goosebumps", "Goosebumps fluff up fur to insulate and look bigger, which helps furry mammals but does little for us.",
      "When you are cold or deeply moved by music, tiny muscles at the base of your hair follicles contract, pulling hairs upright and raising little bumps. Each of these arrector pili muscles attaches to a single hair.\n\nIn heavily furred mammals the same reflex fluffs up the coat, trapping a thicker layer of insulating air. It also makes an animal look bigger, which is why a frightened or angry cat puffs up. Humans have lost most of our body fur, so the reflex achieves very little warmth or intimidation. These muscles may still play a supporting role in hair growth, but the visible goosebump is largely a leftover."],
    ["The tail you once had", "Every human embryo briefly has a tail, and the coccyx that remains still anchors important muscles.",
      "At the base of your spine sits the coccyx, or tailbone, made of several small fused vertebrae. It is what remains of the tail carried by our primate ancestors, and apes such as chimpanzees and gorillas lack an external tail too. Human embryos make the connection vivid. Around the fourth to sixth week of development an embryo has a distinct tail with more vertebrae than an adult keeps. Later the tail stops growing and its extra cells are removed.\n\nYet the coccyx is not useless. It anchors muscles, tendons and ligaments of the pelvic floor, and bears some of your weight when you lean back to sit."],
    ["The appendix rethought", "Once dismissed as useless, the appendix appears to support the immune system and may shelter helpful gut bacteria.",
      "Darwin suggested the appendix was a shrunken remnant of a larger organ that helped ancestral primates digest leaves. For over a century it was treated as a useless troublemaker, famous mainly for appendicitis, which can be life-threatening if the organ bursts.\n\nThe picture has changed. The appendix is rich in lymphoid tissue, involved in immune defence. Researchers have also proposed that it acts as a safe house for beneficial gut bacteria, helping repopulate the intestine after an illness such as severe diarrhoea. Comparative studies suggest an appendix has evolved independently many times in different mammals, which hints at some advantage. Even so, people who have it removed live normal, healthy lives."],
    ["Teeth, muscles and ears", "Wisdom teeth often don't fit because human jaws shrank, and some people lack muscles such as the palmaris longus without any loss.",
      "Our ancestors had larger jaws and chewed tougher food. Human jaws became smaller over hundreds of thousands of years, a change many researchers link to cooking and softer diets. Teeth shrank less, so wisdom teeth often find no room and can stay trapped (impaction), causing pain or infection. A significant minority of people never develop one or more at all.\n\nTouch your thumb to your little finger and flex your wrist: many people see a thin cord rise at the inner wrist. That is the palmaris longus tendon. Roughly one in seven people, varying between populations, lack it in one or both arms, with no noticeable loss of grip strength. Small ear muscles, used by some animals to swivel their ears, survive too, and a few humans can still wiggle theirs."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do biologists mean by a vestigial structure?", ["One that is always completely useless","One that has lost much of its original function","One that only ever appears in embryos","One that grows larger as a person ages"], 1, "Vestigial means reduced or changed in function, and such a part may still have a new job."],
    [1, "What do goosebumps do for furry mammals?", ["Make their skin tighter and waterproof","Help them sweat and cool down in heat","Help them hear sounds from further away","Fluff the fur to insulate and look bigger"], 3, "The reflex traps insulating air and makes an animal look larger, but does little in mostly hairless humans."],
    [2, "What useful job does the coccyx still do?", ["It protects the kidneys from impact","It anchors muscles of the pelvic floor","It controls balance while walking","It carries signals from brain to legs"], 1, "The tailbone is a remnant of a tail, but it supports the pelvic floor and bears weight when sitting."],
    [3, "What role do researchers now propose for the appendix?", ["A safe house for helpful gut bacteria","Digesting the cellulose in plant food","Storing spare fat for lean times","Making most of the digestive enzymes"], 0, "The appendix is rich in immune tissue and may help restock gut bacteria after illness."],
    [4, "Why do wisdom teeth often have no room to arrive?", ["Modern teeth grow faster than before","Wisdom teeth are larger than other molars","Jaws shrank more than teeth did","Baby teeth often fail to fall out"], 2, "Smaller jaws, linked to cooking and softer diets, leave too little space for the third molars."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your body carries a tail, a spare muscle and a gut organ once thought useless."});

WB.L("what-cancer-is", "body", "advanced", "What cancer really is",
  "Cancer is not one disease but hundreds, all sharing a single rebellion: cells that stop following the body's rules. Understanding that rebellion has transformed treatment.",
  [
    ["Hundreds of diseases", "Cancer is hundreds of diseases united by cells that divide without control and invade other tissues.",
      "Cancer is not a modern disease. The Edwin Smith Papyrus, an Egyptian medical text from around 1600 BCE, describes bulging masses in the breast and says there is no treatment. Tumours have also been found in ancient mummies and skeletons. The Greek physician Hippocrates used the word karkinos, meaning crab, perhaps because swollen veins around some tumours looked like a crab's legs. The Romans translated it into the Latin word cancer.\n\nToday we know it is not one disease but hundreds, varying by tissue and by the genes involved. What unites them is cells that divide without control and can invade other tissues."],
    ["Accelerators and brakes", "Cancer begins when mutations damage genes that drive cell growth or genes that restrain it.",
      "Healthy cells divide only when they receive the right signals, and stop when they should. Genes govern this. Proto-oncogenes act like accelerators; when mutated, they can become oncogenes, like an accelerator stuck to the floor. Tumour suppressor genes act like brakes. The most famous, TP53, is called the guardian of the genome because it can halt division or order a damaged cell to destroy itself. It is mutated in around half of all cancers. Elephants, with far more cells than us but no more cancer (a puzzle called Peto's paradox), carry many extra copies of it.\n\nIn 1971 Alfred Knudson, studying a childhood eye cancer, proposed that both copies of a tumour suppressor gene usually need to be knocked out: the two-hit hypothesis."],
    ["Hallmarks and immortality", "Cancer cells gradually acquire shared abilities, including escaping the normal limit on how often a cell can divide.",
      "In 2000 Douglas Hanahan and Robert Weinberg argued that cancers share acquired abilities: making their own growth signals, ignoring stop signals, avoiding programmed cell death, dividing indefinitely, building blood vessels, and invading and spreading. In 2011 they added reprogramming energy use and evading the immune system. No single mutation provides all of these. A cell gathers them over years or decades.\n\nTake dividing indefinitely. In the early 1960s Leonard Hayflick showed normal human cells divide only about 40 to 60 times, partly because protective chromosome caps called telomeres shorten each time. Many cancer cells switch on telomerase, an enzyme that rebuilds the caps. HeLa cells, taken in 1951 from Henrietta Lacks without her knowledge or consent, are still dividing and helped develop the polio vaccine."],
    ["Evolution inside you", "Mutations build up with age and cause cancer, and a tumour evolves, which is why it can resist treatment.",
      "Every cell division carries a small chance of copying errors, and outside factors damage DNA too. Mutations accumulate over a lifetime, the main reason cancer becomes more common with age. Established causes include tobacco smoke (linked to many cancers, not only lung cancer), ultraviolet radiation (behind most skin cancers, a serious issue in sunny Australia), alcohol, and viruses such as human papillomavirus.\n\nA tumour is not a uniform mass. Its cells keep mutating, so one tumour holds many genetically different groups. This is natural selection inside one person: a cell that grows faster or resists a drug leaves more descendants. A therapy may shrink a tumour, then a resistant group regrows, so doctors often combine treatments. Most cancer deaths are caused by tumours that have spread, or metastasised."],
    ["Fighting back", "Treatments now include drugs that target specific mutations or unleash the immune system, and prevention matters too.",
      "Surgery, radiation and chemotherapy remain central, but newer approaches build on understanding how cancer works. Targeted therapies block the specific molecules a cancer depends on. Imatinib, approved in 2001, transformed the outlook for a blood cancer called chronic myeloid leukaemia. Immunotherapy releases the brakes cancers use to hide from immune cells, and James Allison and Tasuku Honjo shared the 2018 Nobel Prize for the discoveries behind these checkpoint drugs.\n\nPrevention matters too. Australia's human papillomavirus vaccination program and cervical screening have put the country on track to eliminate cervical cancer as a public health problem, and national screening programs also look for early breast and bowel cancers."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the word 'cancer' describe today?", ["One disease that behaves the same in every tissue","A modern illness caused by industrial life","Hundreds of diseases sharing uncontrolled growth","An infection that spreads between people"], 2, "Cancers differ by tissue and genes, but all involve cells that divide without control and can invade other tissues."],
    [1, "What happens when a tumour suppressor gene such as TP53 is damaged?", ["A key brake on cell division is lost","The immune system is switched off completely","Telomeres grow longer in every cell","Cells stop dividing and quietly die off"], 0, "Tumour suppressor genes act like brakes, so losing them lets damaged cells keep dividing."],
    [2, "How do many cancer cells avoid the normal limit on cell divisions?", ["They avoid ever being exposed to oxygen","They stop making almost all new proteins","They copy their DNA without any errors","They rebuild their telomeres with telomerase"], 3, "Switching on telomerase rebuilds the protective caps, letting cancer cells divide far beyond the normal limit."],
    [3, "Why can a cancer return after a drug first shrinks the tumour?", ["The drug turns healthy cells into cancer cells","A resistant group of cells survives and regrows","The tumour cells all adapt together at once","The body forgets how to respond to the drug"], 1, "A tumour evolves, so cells that happen to resist a therapy survive and multiply."],
    [4, "What do immunotherapy checkpoint drugs do?", ["Remove tumours by cutting out whole organs","Block the one molecule every cancer depends on","Take the brakes off the immune system","Replace damaged genes in every cell"], 2, "Checkpoint drugs let the immune system recognise and attack cancer cells that had been hiding from it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Cancer is hundreds of diseases. What single rebellion links them all?"});

